<!--
Shared LLM instructions. Keep tool-neutral — portable behavior rules only.
-->

# Instructions

## Collaboration checkpoint

- Checkpoint before continuing: do only the smallest step needed to form a useful 
  answer or hypothesis, then stop and ask me before deeper investigation, workaround, 
  implementation, cleanup, migration, or scope expansion. If anything is unclear, 
  missing, blocked, or has multiple reasonable next steps, ask me instead of deciding alone.

## Asking the user

- When you present choices (AskUserQuestion tool), I must always be able to
  answer in free text instead of picking a predefined option. The harness adds
  a "Type something" free-text entry and a "Chat about this" entry to every
  question automatically — do not add your own free-form option, it is already
  there.
- NEVER use the `preview` field on options (the bordered side-by-side form) —
  it is banned.
- When you ask more than one question at once, add a final extra question
  titled "Extra" (or similar), with exactly two options and no descriptions:
  "Nothing to add" and "—" (a filler, since the tool requires at least two
  options). I write anything extra via the automatic "Type something".

## Reviewing diffs

- When working from the CLI and the `revdiff` skill is available, every diff and
  proposed edit goes to revdiff (TUI overlay) — before I approve it, not after,
  and approval in revdiff is approval to apply that exact change,
  and never printed as text in chat, not even once it was already shown. Text in
  chat is only for asking confirmation. Launch revdiff yourself; I annotate
  whole-file or per-line, and the annotations come back to you as
  `file:line (type)` text. Pass chat context (what to look at, your questions)
  via `--description` so I can read it inside the overlay. Never pipe content into
  the launcher — `--stdin` never reaches the overlay on any backend; write it to a
  file and use `--compare-*` or a temp worktree instead.
- If `$TMUX` is set, launch with `REVDIFF_TMUX_WINDOW=1`: the review then survives
  the launcher being killed at the 600s bash timeout, so that background drop is
  expected — never relaunch. If `$TMUX` is empty, just launch normally and keep the
  timeout in mind; no need to tell me about it.
- To show proposed edits WITHOUT touching real files: one file — `--compare-old=<real,
  resolve symlinks first, else the diff shows every line as added>`
  `--compare-new=<temp copy>`; several files — make them in a temp git worktree and launch
  revdiff from there (with `--untracked`, else new files are invisible).
- Before treating a review as done: a clean launcher exit (code 0, no annotations)
  is approval — unless it returned within a few seconds, which means the overlay
  never opened, so ask. If the launcher was killed at the timeout instead, check the
  newest file under `~/.config/revdiff/history/*/`: a fresh entry whose `path:`
  matches this launch carries my annotations — address them. History is only written
  when annotations exist, so no entry means either a silent approval or no review at
  all — ask.

## Local instructions

For additional local instructions, see following files
- @~/.dotfiles/_local/AGENTS.local.md
